A||||12||SCCA FIERO||Converting from I4 to V6||||14
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:25 AM||||I am going through alot of trouble to put all my posts from seperate threads into one thread about my journey. I hope it helps somebody to do the conversion. I may not have put everything in here that I had trouble with, so if you have questions about something I may be able to remember what I had to do to resolve it. I would do this swap all over again even it was not the funnest thing I have done. <P>This Forum is going to be (if not already) the best place to come for knowledge of the little car that Pontiac turned it's back too right when it was coming together. Cliff Pennock may soon be recognized along with Ed Parks and V8 Archie soon, so I think the trouble of this is worth it.||||reg||14
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:31 AM||||I am trying to get a wiring harness out of my 86 and into my 88.<BR>There are 2 huge connections that go through the firewall. One goes to the engine junction box behind the battery. Can those conections be seperated where they go through the firewall? Do I need the one from my V6 car? The V6 car has more wires running under the rear clip (where do they go?) I need to get the wiring harness out of the car and the only way to get it out is to disconnect that connection. The harness is "trapped" between the support for the steering wheel and the heater casing. The only way it will come out without cutting it in half is to get the connection undone.<P><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:32 AM||||I had a hell of a time getting the main harness out. I had to undo the junction box in the rear next to the battery and pull it through the firewall and then under the brace for the dash. What a bugger. My finger tips are killing me from undoing all those plugs in both cars. I have the throttle cable from the donor car and in the project car. Tomorrow I will finish installing the main harness from the donor into the project car. Hopefully I will also have the V6 and the 4 banger all disconnected and ready to come out.<P>The "cooling fan" you are talking about is where? I have heard of this but have never seen it. I guess that has to be swapped too.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:34 AM||||The 86 dash and gauges are in the 88. The 86 cradle is out and the V6 is seperated from the 4 speed. To get the V6 and 4 speed apart was a pain. The exhaust pipes before the "Y" were keeping the V6 from pulling away from it. There is a big flange on the 4speed that went up behind the pipe and the clutch wouldn't let it lift enough to clear the flange. I had to take a few parts off to get it free and when I did most of the exhaust bolts that bolt the various pipes together broke. <BR>The cradle from the 86 had a bolt that didn't want to come off. On the top and inside the frame is a little bolt type thing that had broke free from the frame and was spinning with the bolt. After 4 Saw-Zall blades,from trying to cut through the bushing and bolt,and 2 hours later I finally cut a hole in the frame to see what was wrong. Finally it was out.<P>I am beat down dead tired.... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:35 AM||||Today I dropped the 88 cradle. Again there was a bolt that didnt want to come out! This time it was one of the front bolts. It would spin,but I hit it with a sledge hammer and it didnt budge an inch. I messed with it for about an hour and finally decided to cut it. I cut it on both sides of the bracket. The cradle was out,but the bolt was still in it. The motor was pulled out of the cradle and I began to drill out the bolt that refuses to come out.<BR>The 4 cylinder exhaust was removed and the V6 exhaust was put into place. Those damn springs are very hard to get on and there are only about 20 of them. I have alot of skin missing thanks to those stupid things. The exhaust is on and everything is ready to be put back in the 88. There is still a long way to go though.<P>The V6 has a little mount that looks like a shock absorber on the front of the engine. The are 3 bolts to mount this to the cradle. The 88 cradle has none. I will have to drill and tap some. The V6 bolted right to the Isuzu 5speed and the motor mount was in the right place for the V6. <P><BR>Here is something I did not know: The 88 cradle has no bushing where it mounts to the frame of the car. It's amazing what you learn when you tear things apart.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:35 AM||||Yesterday I got the cradle back in the car. Today I finished hooking everything up. I then decided it was jugdement time. I pushed in the clutch and turned the key. It fired right up then stopped. It does this everytime I start it. The 4 cylinder fuel tank is still in the car along with the pump and filter. I guess I might have to switch tanks or pumps.<BR>I have electrical problems too. When I turn the key on the fan comes on. When I step on the brake the fan turns off but the dash lights come on. Wipers,headlights,blinkers,horn,a/c and heater controls and all the lights in the headliner work. I have no idea what to do about that. <BR>||||reg||4
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:36 AM||||I found out the I4 only needed 13PSI and the V6 needs about 45PSI. I wonder if the V6 pump will fit in the 88 tank. I guess I will find out tomorrow. <BR>I did pull the rear harness,but I don't know if that would cause the problems I am having. I guess I will put that in too.<P>The 88 fuel lines were right where they needed to be,thank god. The cooling lines are a different story. I spent a whole day getting that to work and I still haven't got that right,but it will work for now. <BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:37 AM||||I got the I4 fuel pump out and the V6 unit in. It runs great,but now a new bigger problem is present. I put it in gear and let out the clutch and the car will not move. It tries to,but it seems like the clutch isn't fully letting go. I used the Isuzu throw-out bearing and the V6 clutch. The clutch was not removed or touched. What could it be? ||||reg||11
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:38 AM||||This post was from <B>lowCG</B><P>Did you check the free play at the clutch arm?<BR>If your clutch is still slipping or whatever it's doing,with the clutch slave cylinder removed,you'll have to separate the package again.<BR>Can you start it in gear,with the slave cyl. removed,and see if it hooks up? ||||reg||3
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:39 AM||||Is it possible that the Isuzu throwout bearing just won't work with the V6 clutch?<BR>I removed the slave and that isn't stopping the arm from moving all the way back. <BR>||||reg||4
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:39 AM||||I discovered that the clutch I used was the wrong one. I used the clutch from the V6,I was supposed to use the clutch for the tranny. This kinda makes me feel kinda stupid for doing that,but I build houses not cars. <BR>The cradle was dropped once again. This time the correct clutch was installed. I have to get the air out of the clutch then I will know if it works. After 9 days of non-stop working on this thing I have had enough. Today I REST.<P>I still have to install the rear wiring harness and hope that will fix my electrical problems. <P>||||reg||6
Z||000011||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:40 AM||||After dropping the cradle and installing the wrong clutch and dropping it again I am finally driving the beast. This swap wasn't a walk in the park,but I think it's worth it. I had electrical trouble too,but I installed the rear harness from the 86 and EVERYTHING works. <P>All the above posts were taken from this thread...  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000360.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000360.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 09-12-1999).]||||reg||14
Z||000012||SCCA FIERO||09-12-1999||06:51 AM||||<B>This post was to a reply about someone wanting to put the V6 into an 88 Coupe which is exactly what I did. The 88 caradle is different than the others and this only applies to the 88 cars.</B><P>You will need a new front engine mount for an 88 V6 Fiero or extend the foot on the original mount about 2 inches. You will also need to drill the 88 cradle to mount the motor strut. (Looks like a tiny shock) The 88's didn't not use this. If you don't do these two things your motor will sit crooked.<BR>If you have the complete donor car things will go smooth. First thing I did was install the 86 interior in my 88. The 4 cylinder gauge pod will not interchange with the V6 gauges in any way. Then while the dash was out I installed the V6 wiring harness. The harness is hard to wiggle into place but it is possible. MAKE SURE TO PUT IN THE V6 THROTTLE CABLE WHILE YOU HAVE IT RIPPED APART.  <P>This post was taken from this thread....  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000611.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000611.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 09-12-1999).]||||reg||1
